@implements IDisposable
@inject SimulatedSessionProvider Session
@inject LocaleService L
@inject NavigationManager Navigation

@* The session bar — who is signed in, on every screen, at every breakpoint.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------
   This is where the sidebar's `nav-session` block went, and the move is the
   point. The reasoning that put the indicator in the sidebar was right — with
   two demo records whose whole point is comparison, "which one am I looking at"
   is a question the interface has to answer without being asked — but the
   sidebar collapses on a phone, so the one place the question is hardest to
   answer was the one place nothing answered it. A row above the content column
   does not collapse.

   THE INDICATOR IS NOT A LINK, and that is carried over deliberately: it is a
   statement of fact, not a door. The door is the nav row below it. Everything
   inside the signed-in branch is text, which is what keeps the read-only
   property the retired block had and the tests still assert.

   THE NAME IS GENERIC, DELIBERATELY. Both demo records carry a persona name
   internally — the record and its own tests need one, and changing it there
   would touch data outside this run's scope — but nothing on screen shows
   either. The credential ID differentiates the two holders without naming
   which is which, which is all the bar ever needed to demonstrate: which
   record you are looking at, not who it belongs to.

   Signed out it holds exactly one thing: a way in. It points at `/record`
   rather than at a sign-in route of its own, because `/record` is the
   conditional surface that renders the sign-in experience to a visitor with no
   session and the record to one with a session.

   Sign-out appears here and in Settings, and that is not a duplication defect:
   a duplicated status indicator can disagree with itself, a duplicated action
   cannot. Both call the same method and navigate to the same place. *@

<div class="session-bar">
    @if (Session.Current is DemoAccount holder)
    {
        <div class="session-bar__holder">
            <span class="session-bar__label">@L[Locale, "nav.signedInAs"]</span>
            <span class="session-bar__name">@L[Locale, "record.genericHolderName"]</span>
            <span class="session-bar__credential">@holder.CredentialId</span>
        </div>
        <button type="button" class="session-bar__signout" @onclick="SignOut">
            @L[Locale, "nav.signOut"]
        </button>
    }
    else
    {
        @* A button, in the same outline register as the match list's "View
           details" — it is a way somewhere, not a sentence, and an underlined
           link in a bar of chrome read as body text that happened to be blue. *@
        <NavLink class="btn btn-outline-primary btn-sm session-bar__signin" href="record">
            @L[Locale, "nav.signIn"]
        </NavLink>
    }
</div>

@code {
    [CascadingParameter]
    public AppLocale Locale { get; set; }

    protected override void OnInitialized() => Session.OnChanged += Refresh;

    public void Dispose() => Session.OnChanged -= Refresh;

    /// <summary>
    /// The session changes from a form this component does not own and cannot
    /// be told about directly. Subscribing is what keeps the bar from being a
    /// render behind the thing it reports.
    /// </summary>
    private void Refresh() => InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);

    private void SignOut()
    {
        Session.SignOut();

        // Back to the public landing, which announces that the sign-out
        // happened rather than leaving a person to infer it from a bar that
        // quietly changed shape. Identical to Settings' own sign-out.
        Navigation.NavigateTo("");
    }
}
